  "answer_text": "The episode you are looking for is titled **\"Yuppy Love\"**. It is the first episode of **Series 6** and originally aired on **January 8, 1989**. \n\nThis scene is widely considered one of the funniest and most iconic moments in the history of British television comedy.\n\n---\n\n### The Scene\nDel Boy (David Jason) has adopted a new \"yuppie\" lifestyle\u2014complete with a striped shirt, red braces, a filofax, and a silver briefcase\u2014inspired by the movie *Wall Street*. \n\nTo show off his new sophisticated persona, Del and Trigger (Roger Lloyd-Pack) ditch their usual pub, the Nag's Head, to drink at a trendy, upmarket wine bar. Spotting two attractive women across the room, Del decides to play it cool. He leans against the bar, trying to act suave, and tells Trigger how to handle \"modern women\":\n\n> **Del Boy:** *\"I think we're on a winner here, Trig. Play it nice and cool, son, nice and cool, you know what I mean?\"*\n\nWhile Del turns away to lock eyes with one of the women, the bartender lifts the bar flap to walk through. Del, unaware of this, turns back to lean on the bar flap and tumbles straight through the open hatch, disappearing entirely from view.\n\nAdding to the comedy, Trigger is looking the other way when it happens. When he turns back, Del is gone, and Trigger is left looking around in utter confusion, wondering where on earth his friend vanished to. Del then pops his head back up from behind the bar, trying to salvage his dignity, and advises: *\"Keep an eye on the glasses, Trig.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Behind-the-Scenes Facts\n\n* **Trigger wasn\u2019t supposed to be there:** A rehearsal script auctioned in 2023 revealed that Roger Lloyd-Pack's character, Trigger, was not originally written into the scene. He happened to turn up on set that day, and writer John Sullivan decided to write him in. Trigger's completely oblivious, delayed reaction ended up being one of the funniest parts of the joke.\n* **Real-life inspiration:** John Sullivan got the idea for the stunt after visiting a pub and watching a man nearly fall through a bar hatch in the exact same manner. Sullivan realized it would make a perfect gag for Del Boy.\n* **Filming Locations:** The exterior of the wine bar used in the episode was **The Granary** on Queen Charlotte Street in Bristol. The actual fall itself was filmed on a constructed studio set to ensure David Jason could fall safely.\n* **It was filmed in one take:** Because of how difficult it was to coordinate the physical stunt safely, David Jason managed to execute the perfect fall on the very first take.\n\n### What else happens in the episode?\nAside from Del trying to be a yuppie, \"Yuppy Love\" is a monumental episode for the series because **Rodney meets Cassandra** (Gwyneth Strong) for the first time. Rodney joins an evening computer class where he meets Cassandra, but, embarrassed by his working-class background and the council flat in Nelson Mandela House, he pretends he lives on a luxurious, expensive street.",
